<div class="key-takeaways reveal">
  <h2>Key Takeaways</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Follow the arrow:</strong> if you hit low, move the sight down; if you hit left, move it left. Move the pin (or the whole sight) toward the miss.</li>
    <li>Rough in at <strong>10 yards</strong>, then set your <strong>20-yard pin</strong> with the whole housing (gang adjustment) before touching any other pin.</li>
    <li>Set 30, 40, 50 and 60 one pin at a time (gap adjustment), shooting groups, not single arrows.</li>
    <li>Level the sight on all three axes, or your misses will drift sideways at long range and on uphill or downhill shots.</li>
    <li>Before hunting, confirm that your <strong>broadheads hit with your field points</strong>. If they don&rsquo;t, tune the bow, not the sight.</li>
  </ul>
</div>

<p class="lead">Learning how to sight in a compound bow is the step that turns a new setup into a bow you trust. The process is the same for any brand: start close, set one reference pin, then build every other yardage from it. Once you understand the one rule that drives it all, follow the arrow, sighting in a bow takes an afternoon and a few dozen arrows.</p>

<p>This Better Outdoors guide walks you through each step: safety, the 10-yard rough-in, the 20-yard pin, gang and gap adjustments, setting 30 through 60 yards, 2nd and 3rd axis leveling, peep alignment and the final broadhead check. It works for multi-pin sights and single pin sliders alike.</p>

<h2 id="h-before-you-start-safety-and-setup-checks">Before You Start: Safety and Setup Checks</h2>
<p>Sighting in only works if the rest of the bow is consistent. A sight can&rsquo;t fix a draw length that changes, a loose rest or a peep that spins. Spend ten minutes on these checks first.</p>
<ul>
  <li><strong>Draw length and draw weight are final.</strong> Changing either later moves every pin. If you are still adjusting, read our guide on <?= post_link('how-to-adjust-draw-length', 'how to adjust draw length') ?> first.</li>
  <li><strong>Everything is tight.</strong> Check sight mount screws, rest screws, the quiver mount and the stabilizer. Use a little blue thread locker on the sight bracket if it keeps backing out.</li>
  <li><strong>The peep and D-loop are set.</strong> Your peep should come to your eye with your head in a natural position (more on that below).</li>
  <li><strong>Your arrows are matched.</strong> Same shaft, spine, point weight and nocks. Number them to spot a stray.</li>
  <li><strong>You have a safe backstop.</strong> A target rated for your bow&rsquo;s speed, a clear area behind it, and nobody downrange.</li>
</ul>
<?= callout('warn', 'Never dry-fire', 'Always shoot a full-weight arrow. Dry-firing a compound bow (releasing the string with no arrow) can destroy cams, limbs and strings and can injure you. If you let down instead of shooting, ease the string forward under control.') ?>
<p>Bring a set of hex keys that fit your sight, a pencil, and a notepad or phone for recording yardages. A simple <?= amz('archery allen wrench set bow tool', 'archery hex key set') ?> saves a lot of rounded screw heads.</p>

<h2 id="h-the-golden-rule-follow-the-arrow">The Golden Rule of Sighting In a Bow: Follow the Arrow</h2>
<p>Every sight adjustment comes back to one rule: <strong>move the sight in the same direction as your miss.</strong> Archers call this &ldquo;chasing&rdquo; or &ldquo;following&rdquo; the arrow.</p>
<?= data_table(
