        ['Right', 'Right', 'Moving the sight right makes you swing the bow left'],
    ],
    'Follow-the-arrow sight adjustments'
) ?>
<p>It feels backward at first, but you are moving the aiming point, not the arrow.</p>
<?= callout('tip', 'Judge groups, not single arrows', 'Shoot three to five arrows before you adjust. One flyer tells you about your form; a group tells you about your sight. Adjust to the center of the group and ignore obvious bad shots you called as you released.') ?>

<h2 id="h-step-1-rough-in-at-10-yards">Step 1: Rough In at 10 Yards</h2>
<p>Start close so you can&rsquo;t miss the target. A new sight can be several inches off, and 10 yards keeps every arrow on the bag.</p>
<ol>
  <li>Center the sight housing on its windage and elevation travel so you have room to adjust both ways.</li>
  <li>Stand at 10 yards and aim your top pin at the center of the target.</li>
  <li>Shoot a group of three.</li>
  <li>Follow the arrow: move the whole sight housing (or the whole pin block) up, down, left or right toward the group.</li>
  <li>Repeat until your top pin hits close to center.</li>
</ol>

<h2 id="h-step-2-set-your-20-yard-pin-gang-adjustment">Step 2: Set Your 20-Yard Pin With a Gang Adjustment</h2>
<p>The 20-yard pin is your anchor point for the entire sight. Get it exactly right before touching any other pin.</p>
<p>Move back to a measured 20 yards (use a tape or a rangefinder, not paces). Aim your top pin at the X and shoot groups. Now you make a <strong>gang adjustment</strong>: move the entire housing or pin block, not the individual pin. Gang adjustments move every pin together, which keeps the spacing between pins intact.</p>
<ul>
  <li>Use gang adjustments for left/right (windage) on every distance. Windage is set once, at 20 yards, for all pins.</li>
  <li>Use gang adjustments for up/down at 20 yards so the top pin hits center.</li>
  <li>Shoot until three groups in a row land in the center. Then lock the housing.</li>
</ul>
<p>Once the 20-yard pin is set, don&rsquo;t touch the gang adjustment again unless your 20-yard pin goes off. If you move it, every other pin moves too.</p>

<h2 id="h-step-3-gap-adjust-30-40-50-and-60">Step 3: Gap-Adjust Your 30, 40, 50 and 60-Yard Pins</h2>
<p>Now use <strong>gap adjustments</strong>: move individual pins inside the housing to set each distance. This is the &ldquo;gang and gap&rdquo; method that most pro shops use.</p>
<ol>
  <li>Back up to 30 yards. Aim with your second pin and shoot a group.</li>
  <li>Loosen only that pin and move it toward the group (follow the arrow, vertical only).</li>
  <li>Repeat until it groups in the center, then tighten it.</li>
  <li>Do the same at 40, 50 and 60 yards with the next pins down.</li>
</ol>
<p>Your pins will get farther apart as distance increases. That is the arrow&rsquo;s arc: the farther it travels, the more it drops per yard. A faster arrow drops less, so its pins sit closer together; a heavier, slower arrow spreads the pins out.</p>
