<p>Arrow speed is why your buddy&rsquo;s pin gaps look nothing like yours. Estimate your real speed before you start moving pins.</p>

<h2 id="h-single-pin-slider-sights-and-sight-tapes">Single Pin Slider Sights and Sight Tapes</h2>
<p>A single pin slider works the same way, just with one pin that moves on an elevation track. You still set 20 yards with the housing (windage and elevation), then set a second distance, often 50 or 60 yards, by moving the slider.</p>
<p>Most sliders use a printed <strong>sight tape</strong> that matches yardage marks to your arrow&rsquo;s trajectory. You set two known distances, then pick the tape that lines up with both marks, or enter your numbers into the sight maker&rsquo;s tape program. After that, confirm every yardage you plan to shoot. Tapes are calculated, so a quick check at 30 and 40 yards catches small errors.</p>
<p>Many western hunters like sliders because one pin keeps the sight picture clean at long range. Whitetail hunters often stick with fixed pins because nothing has to move when a deer appears. If you want to try one, look at a <?= amz('single pin slider bow sight', 'single pin slider bow sight') ?> with clear yardage marks and a positive lock.</p>

<h2 id="h-level-your-sight-2nd-and-3rd-axis">Level Your Sight: 1st, 2nd and 3rd Axis Leveling</h2>
<p>A sight that is not level makes misses that look like form problems. Leveling happens on three axes:</p>
<ul>
  <li><strong>1st axis:</strong> the bubble level is square to the pins and the sight is plumb when the bow is plumb. Set this first.</li>
  <li><strong>2nd axis:</strong> the bubble stays centered when you tilt the bow forward and back, like aiming up or down. Off 2nd axis, your arrows drift sideways on steep shots from a treestand or on a mountain slope.</li>
  <li><strong>3rd axis:</strong> the bubble stays centered when you rotate the bow toward you at full draw. Off 3rd axis, your long-range pins drift left or right even though 20 yards is perfect.</li>
</ul>
<p>The cleanest way to set 2nd and 3rd axis is in a bow vise with a string level, or at a pro shop. A quick field check: if your 20-yard pin is centered but your 60-yard group always lands to one side, suspect 3rd axis.</p>
<p>Check the bubble on every shot. Even a few degrees of cant pushes arrows off line, more so at distance.</p>

<h2 id="h-peep-alignment-and-consistent-anchor">Peep Alignment and a Consistent Anchor</h2>
<p>Your peep sight is the rear sight. If it moves, your pins move. When you come to full draw with your eyes closed and then open them, you should be looking straight through the peep at the housing without moving your head.</p>
<ul>
  <li>Center the sight housing (or the scope ring) inside the peep the same way every time.</li>
  <li>Keep the same anchor: hand position, face contact and nose-to-string touch.</li>
  <li>If the peep comes back crooked, the string may need twisting or a peep tube. Get it fixed before sighting in, because a twisting peep changes your aim.</li>
</ul>
<p>Peep height is tied to draw length. That is one reason we always tell readers to finalize draw length first. Owners of older bows will find the specific cam steps in our <?= post_link('2016-pse-draw-length-adjustment', '2016 PSE draw length adjustment guide') ?>.</p>

<h2 id="h-broadhead-tuning-vs-field-points">Broadhead Tuning vs. Field Points: The Final Check</h2>
<p>Field points and broadheads of the same weight should hit together. When they don&rsquo;t, the broadheads are magnifying a tuning problem. Fixed blades steer like small wings, so any arrow that leaves the bow crooked shows up as a broadhead miss.</p>
<ol>
  <li>Shoot a group of field points at 20 yards. Mark the center.</li>
  <li>Shoot one broadhead at a separate spot on a broadhead target, aiming the same pin at the same mark size.</li>
  <li>Repeat at 30 and 40 yards if your broadheads stay close.</li>
</ol>
<p>If the broadheads hit with the field points, you are done. If they consistently hit in a different spot, <strong>move the rest, not the sight.</strong> The common shop rule is the opposite of the sight rule: move the rest away from the broadhead impact (broadheads left, move the rest right; broadheads high, move the rest down). Small moves, about 1/16 inch at a time.</p>
<?= callout('note', 'Use a dedicated broadhead target', 'Broadheads can ruin a bag target and are hard to pull. A layered foam <a class="amz" href="' . e(amz_url('broadhead target foam block')) . '" target="_blank" rel="nofollow sponsored noopener">broadhead target</a> saves your practice target and your blades.') ?>
<p>Mechanical broadheads usually fly closer to field points because their blades stay folded in flight, but you should still confirm them before season. At Better Outdoors we recommend a final broadhead check after any change to your rest, string or arrows.</p>

