<h2 id="h-common-mistakes-when-you-sight-in-a-compound-bow">Common Mistakes When You Sight In a Compound Bow</h2>
<ul>
  <li><strong>Adjusting after every arrow.</strong> You end up chasing form errors around the target.</li>
  <li><strong>Moving the gang after setting 20 yards.</strong> It shifts every pin you already set.</li>
  <li><strong>Skipping the broadhead check.</strong> A perfect field-point sight-in doesn&rsquo;t guarantee broadhead accuracy.</li>
  <li><strong>Forgetting to re-check.</strong> Strings stretch, screws loosen and bows get bumped in trucks. Confirm your 20-yard pin before every hunt.</li>
</ul>

<h2 id="h-final-thoughts">Final Thoughts</h2>
<p>How to sight in a compound bow comes down to a short, repeatable routine: get the bow consistent, rough in at 10, nail 20 with the whole housing, gap the rest, level the sight and finish with broadheads. Write your yardages down, re-check before each season, and you will always know where your arrow is going. Better Outdoors has more setup guides in the Bow Tuning section if you want to keep dialing in your rig.</p>

